/F1 18 Tf (OpsPulse Incident Runbook: Kubernetes Ingress 504 Timeouts) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
T*
/F1 14 Tf (1. Incident Description & Scope) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
(Symptoms: Ingress-Nginx returns HTTP 504 Gateway Timeout to external clients.) Tj T*
(Upstream service pods fail to respond within the configured proxy-read-timeout.) Tj T*
(Log Pattern: [error] upstream timed out \(110: Connection timed out\) while reading response header.) Tj T*
(Affected Components: Ingress Controller, API Gateway, Upstream Microservices, Service Mesh.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf (2. Root Cause Diagnostics Protocol) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
(Step 1: Check Ingress Controller error logs for timeout spikes and upstream IP addresses:) Tj T*
(  kubectl logs -n ingress-nginx -l app.kubernetes.io/name=ingress-nginx --tail=200 | grep 504) Tj T*
(Step 2: Inspect upstream service endpoints and verify pod health:) Tj T*
(  kubectl get endpoints order-service -n production) Tj T*
(Step 3: Check CPU and memory saturation on worker pods:) Tj T*
(  kubectl top pods -n production -l app=order-service) Tj T*
(Step 4: Check if upstream pods are thread starved or waiting on downstream database pool.) Tj T*
T*
/F1 14 Tf (3. Emergency Remediation & Fix Actions) Tj T* /F1 11 Tf
(Action A \(Immediate Scale Out\): Increase replicas to distribute request load immediately:) Tj T*
(  kubectl scale deployment order-service -n production --replicas=10) Tj T*
(Action B \(Temporary Timeout Increase\): Annotate ingress with extended timeout during incident:) Tj T*
(  nginx.ingress.kubernetes.io/proxy-read-timeout: 120) Tj T*
(Action C \(Graceful Rollout\): Restart blocked pods exhibiting deadlocks or high garbage collection:) Tj T*
(  kubectl rollout restart deployment/order-service -n production) Tj T*
ET
endstream
endobj
